<article class="env-definition" id="0644">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0644">Definition <span data-tag="0644">13.19.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category. Let $A \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{A})$. A <em>projective resolution of $A$</em> is a complex $P^\bullet $ together with a map $P^0 \to A$ such that: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We have $P^ n = 0$ for $n > 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Each $P^ n$ is a projective object of $\mathcal{A}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The map $P^0 \to A$ induces an isomorphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(d^{-1}) \to A$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We have $H^ i(P^\bullet ) = 0$ for $i < 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Hence $P^\bullet \to A[0]$ is a quasi-isomorphism. In other words the complex </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \ldots \to P^{-1} \to P^0 \to A \to 0 \to \ldots  \]
</div>
  <p> is acyclic. Let $K^\bullet $ be a complex in $\mathcal{A}$. A <em>projective resolution of $K^\bullet $</em> is a complex $P^\bullet $ together with a map $\alpha : P^\bullet \to K^\bullet $ of complexes such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We have $P^ n = 0$ for $n \gg 0$, i.e., $P^\bullet $ is bounded above. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Each $P^ n$ is a projective object of $\mathcal{A}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The map $\alpha : P^\bullet \to K^\bullet $ is a quasi-isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>